  "articleBody": "Getting a clear picture of your roof's remaining life can feel like a big step, but a little preparation on your end can make the whole assessment much smoother and more accurate. Think of it like a doctor's visit for your house – the more history you can provide, the better the diagnosis. This isn't just about saving time; it helps us understand the full context of your roof's journey, from its initial installation to any repairs it's had over the years. Knowing these details upfront helps us give you the most honest and reliable assessment possible, ensuring we don't miss any critical information that could impact our findings. We're all about clarity and straightforward communication, and that starts with good information.\n\n### What to Prepare Before You Start\n\n- [ ] **Original Installation Documents** — These could be the original contract, invoice, or builder's notes indicating the type of roofing material, manufacturer, and installation date. This helps verify the initial quality and expected lifespan.\n- [ ] **Prior Repair Records** — Any invoices or work orders from past roof repairs, including dates, areas repaired, and materials used. This shows what work has been done and where potential weak spots might be.\n- [ ] **Maintenance History** — Records of any routine maintenance, cleaning, or inspections. A well-maintained roof generally lasts longer.\n- [ ] **Warranty Information** — Details of any existing manufacturer or contractor warranties. This can be important for potential claims or understanding coverage.\n- [ ] **Known Leak Locations** — Any notes or photos of past or current leaks, even if they seemed minor. This helps us pinpoint problematic areas quickly.\n\n### How to use this checklist\nGathering these items before your assessment will give your roofing professional a head start in understanding your roof’s history and condition, leading to a more precise life expectancy estimate. Don't worry if you don't have everything; any information you can provide is helpful.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-02"
}